Common Complex Kubernetes Issues

✔ Pods stuck in CrashLoopBackOff
Usually caused by application crashes, wrong environment variables, or incorrect command/entrypoint configuration.

✔ ImagePullBackOff errors
Often due to incorrect image tags, missing imagePullSecrets, or registry authentication failures.

✔ Pods in Pending state
Caused by insufficient node resources, incorrect node selectors, taints, or affinity rules.

✔ Service not reachable
Even when pods are running, misconfigured Services, wrong target ports, or missing endpoints can block traffic.

✔ Intermittent traffic issues
Frequently related to readiness/liveness probes or misconfigured Ingress rules.

🔹 My Troubleshooting Approach
1️⃣ Start with Pod & Event inspection
kubectl get pods
kubectl describe pod <pod-name>
2️⃣ Check application logs
kubectl logs <pod-name>
Previous crash logs when required
3️⃣ Validate configuration
Environment variables
Resource limits & requests
Probes configuration
4️⃣ Check node-level issues
Node pressure (CPU, memory, disk)
Pod scheduling constraints
5️⃣ Verify networking components
Service endpoints
Ingress controller logs
Network policies

🔹 Key Tools & Commands Used
kubectl describe
kubectl logs
kubectl exec


Kubernetes troubleshooting is not about memorizing YAML—
it’s about understanding how the system behaves under failure.
